{"name":"Alpha Centauri","hex":"#4d5778","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Alpha Centauri","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The brightest star in the southern constellation of Centaurus. It is actually a binary star with Proxima Centauri as a third component."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Alpha_Centauri"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alpha Centauri","blocks":[{"text":"Alpha Centauri is a star system in the southern constellation of Centaurus. It consists of three stars: Rigil Kentaurus, Toliman, and Proxima Centauri. Proxima Centauri is the closest star to the Sun at 4.2465 light-years (ly), which is 1.3020 parsecs (pc), while Alpha Centauri A and B are the nearest stars visible to the naked eye."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/The_sky_around_Alpha_Centauri_and_Proxima_Centauri_%28eso1629i%29.jpg/330px-The_sky_around_Alpha_Centauri_and_Proxima_Centauri_%28eso1629i%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Centauri","wikidata":"Q12176"},{"title":"Alpha Centauri (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Alpha Centauri is an Irish Thoroughbred racehorse. In 2018 she recorded four consecutive Group 1 races, namely the Irish 1,000 Guineas, Coronation Stakes, Falmouth Stakes and Prix Jacques Le Marois."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Owner_Niarchos_Family.svg/330px-Owner_Niarchos_Family.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Centauri_(horse)","wikidata":"Q55974119"},{"title":"Alpha","blocks":[{"text":"Alpha ALF-ə is the first letter of the Greek alphabet. In the system of Greek numerals, it has a value of one. Alpha is derived from the Phoenician letter aleph ⟨𐤀⟩, whose name comes from the West Semitic word for 'ox'. Letters that arose from alpha include the Latin letter ⟨A⟩ and the Cyrillic letter ⟨А⟩."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha","wikidata":"Q9887"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/The_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg/500px-The_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/The_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg/1280px-The_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/The_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg/500px-The_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Very Large Telescope open to the night sky, with the Milky Way running diagonally across the sky above it and many southern stars and constellations labelled and connected by lines, including Alpha Centauri and the not visible Proxima Centauri.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Very_Large_Telescope_and_the_star_system_Alpha_Centauri.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg/500px-Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg/1280px-Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg/500px-Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alpha Centauri AB (left) 